[gd_scene load_steps=105 format=3]

; CORAL COVE — the first official world (REQ-011), and a full platforming
; playthrough: run, jump, swim, dive, boost and climb, with the difficulty
; rising across seven acts, in a forested river valley.
;
; Built the way an outside contributor would build one: manifest + scene, NO
; scripts. Every mechanic below is a DECLARATION the game client's
; WorldSystems runtime binds — group membership plus node metadata, never
; code. The Sanctioned World API Surface is not called here because nothing
; here calls anything.
;
; GEOMETRY IS DERIVED FROM MEASUREMENT, not from taste. The controller was
; driven headlessly through the greybox against the shipped tuning and
; reported:
;
;     run 4.20 m/s | standing jump 1.81 m | running jump 3.15 m of gap
;     climb 2.0 m/s to a 6.0 m ceiling | swim 6.0 m/s | bubble boost x2.2
;
; Every mandatory gap on the route is 2.6 m or less against that 3.15 m, and
; every mandatory rise 1.2 m or less against that 1.81 m — comfortable, but
; not free. The one jump built to the edge of the envelope (1.6 m of rise
; across 3.0 m) is the OPTIONAL one, onto ShrimpLedge. If the arc is ever
; retuned smaller, this level stops being completable, and the coral walk is
; what says so.
;
; THE ROUTE, in seven acts down -Z:
;
;   ACT 1  z   4 → -29   THE TIDE SHELF. Land. A rising staircase of four
;                        gaps, teaching run-and-jump on wide, forgiving
;                        platforms. The hermit snail (a discovery
;                        collectible) sits on the third.
;   ACT 2  z -30 → -50   THE COVE. The river widens and deepens, and the
;                        grammar flipped on entry back in act 1. Two
;                        obstacles that cannot be swum straight through: a
;                        rock brow passable only UNDERNEATH, then a shelf
;                        passable only OVER — down, then up, in one
;                        crossing. The bubble boost is what makes it quick.
;   ACT 3  z -50 → -67   THE CORAL WALL. Climb. Four metres of climbable
;                        face inside the 6 m ceiling. The block is both the
;                        wall and the platform above it, so clearing the lip
;                        ends the climb by running out of wall.
;   ACT 4  z -67 → -95   THE GLOW GROTTO. The hard section. The Glow gate
;                        first, then four narrow pillars over open water,
;                        two of them offset sideways so the jump has to be
;                        aimed and not merely timed. The stray hook waits on
;                        the third and strips a leg — an ORDINARY hazard,
;                        which never costs a life. Then the Bubble gate.
;   ACT 5  z -95 → -119  THE SEED BEDS. Three descending terraces carrying
;                        the seven kelp seeds; two of them out on side
;                        pillars that cost a jump each way. The regen
;                        station regrows the leg on the way in.
;   ACT 6  z-119 → -126  THE SHELF. The restoration gate opens only when
;                        coral_shelf reaches `restored`, so restoration is
;                        MANDATORY, then the finish volume returns the
;                        player to the Open Lagoon.
;
;   ACT 4b z -98 → -108  THE GORGE RIVER. A swim dropped into the middle of
;                        the jumping, so the grammar switch lands while the
;                        player is still in a jumping rhythm. A rock rib
;                        across the slot has to be dived under, and the slot
;                        walls stand six metres back from the landing — twice
;                        the jump — so there is no bank to hop along instead.
;
; THE VALLEY. The route hangs in a forested river valley rather than over
; open water, and that is gameplay rather than scenery: a jump over nothing
; gives the player no way to judge height or distance. A forest floor 24 m
; below carries ferns and fallen logs, canopy trees rise past the route and
; close overhead, and the valley's sides show in the haze.
;
; WATER IS THE FORGIVING HALF, and it is placed on purpose. A river runs
; under the whole tide shelf, so a missed jump in act 1 is a swim downstream
; and never a life; reeds mark that waterline. The glow grotto's four pillars
; have no water under them at all. That contrast is the difficulty curve
; stated in geometry rather than in a comment.
;
; UNDER EVERYTHING: one pit volume, BETWEEN the route and the forest floor.
; Every gap the waterway does not run under is a real one — falling costs a
; life and returns the player to the last checkpoint (REQ-003) — and the
; floor below is scenery with no collider, so the player watches the valley
; come up at them rather than landing in it.
;
; SURFACES. Nothing here carries a colour of its own: platforms wear the
; client's shared procedural terrain materials (triplanar world-space noise
; over a stratum term, so no surface needs UVs and the grain runs ACROSS the
; seam where two boxes meet). The two mod gates are the deliberate exception
; and keep a flat colour, because a gate is a rule and not geology.
;
; CHECKPOINTS follow REQ-003 AC-7: the replay from any anchor to the next
; must fit progression.max_retry_seconds. A platforming route is slower per
; metre than a walk, so they are DENSER than the old corridor's — thirteen
; of them — and the coral walk MEASURES every segment rather than trusting
; this comment.
;
; This file is generated geometry: the coordinates were emitted from the
; measured envelope above rather than typed, which is why the gaps are exact
; numbers and not round ones.

